# SOURCES - the kernel sources that EVERY target compiles.
#
# There were four copies of this list: build.sh (32-bit multiboot), build64.sh
# (64-bit multiboot), buildefi.sh (UEFI application, clang/LLP64) and mkdisk.sh
# (our own bootloader). Adding one .c file to the one you were testing with and
# forgetting the other three broke the UEFI and raw builds TWICE on 2026-08-18,
# and verify.sh cannot see it because verify.sh only builds the first.
#
# Anything added here is compiled into all four. `./verify-sources.sh` proves
# that by adding a throwaway file and looking for it in all four outputs, so
# the claim is checked rather than asserted.
#
# WHAT IS NOT HERE, and why. Four things genuinely differ per target, and they
# are structural rather than a list that drifts - a new driver is added here, a
# new ENTRY POINT is a new port:
#
#   the generated C   _gen.c / _gen64.c / _genefi.c  (../compile kernel.zl)
#   the GDT           gdt.c (32-bit) / gdt64.c (64-bit, and the EFI build)
#   the AP trampoline smp_trampoline.S / smp_trampoline64.S
#   the entry point   boot.S / boot64.S / raw_entry.S / efi.c
#
# freestanding/runtime_kernel.c is also per-target: three scripts compile it
# with -DZL_KERNEL_SERIAL alone, while buildefi.sh applies that define to every
# file in its loop. Folding it in here would bake that asymmetry into all four.
#
# Order is the order they are compiled and linked. It does not matter to the
# linker; it is kept as it was so a diff against the old scripts is readable.

